1. Starting a ProjectThese commands get you up and running with a repository.git init: Transforms the current directory into a Git repository. It creates a hidden .git folder where all your version history is stored.git clone <url>: Downloads an existing repository from a remote server (like GitHub) to your local machine
. It automatically sets up a connection to the original source.2.
The Daily Workflow (Stage & Commit)Git uses a three-tier system: your Working Directory (files), the Staging Area (the "loading dock"), and the Repository (the permanent record).
git status: The most important command. It tells you which files have changed, what is staged for the next commit, and what Git is ignoring.git add <file>: Moves changes from your directory to the Staging Area. Use git add . to stage all changes at once.
git commit -m "message": Takes everything in the staging area and "saves" it as a permanent snapshot in the project history.3.
Branching and MergingBranches allow you to work on new features without breaking the "Main" code.git branch: Lists all local branches.git branch <name>: 
Creates a new branch at your current location.git checkout <branch>: Switches your working files to the specified branch. (In newer versions, git switch <branch> is preferred).git checkout -b <name>: A shortcut that creates a new branch and switches to it immediately.git merge <branch>: Combines the history of the specified branch into your current one.4. Working with RemotesThis is how you collaborate with others.git remote add origin <url>: Connects your local repository to a specific online URL.git push <remote> <branch>: Uploads your local commits to the online server.git pull: The opposite of push. It fetches updates from the server and merges them into your current local branch.git fetch: Downloads updates from the server but does not merge them. It’s a "safe" way to see what others have done before you commit to changing your own files.5. Reviewing and UndoingBecause we all make mistakes.git log: Shows a chronological list of all commits made in the repository.git diff: Shows the exact line-by-line changes between your files and the last commit.git reset --hard <commit>: Caution! This wipes out all uncommitted changes and returns your project to the state of a specific previous commit.git revert <commit>: A "safer" undo. Instead of deleting history, it creates a new commit that does the exact opposite of a previous commit.git stash: Temporarily "hides" your uncommitted changes so you can switch branches without committing unfinished work. You can bring them back later with git stash pop.Quick Reference TableCommandPurposeinitCreate a new repostatusSee what's happeningaddPrepare files for savingcommitSave the snapshotpushSend to GitHub/GitLabpullGet updates from otherslogSee the history
